#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134881} :activity/id #uuid "1129f5af-8b20-5759-9f3e-97b087634b1b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049353 :topic/id #uuid "ee26b28d-c213-5819-bf4f-97826f641d1d" :topic/math-academy-id 2148} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134881 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135013 :tutorial/content "**Periodic functions** are special functions that repeat themselves every $c$ units. The smallest positive value of $c$ that has this property is called the **period** of the function.\n\nLet's take the following periodic function as an example:\n\n![](images/f4/f483c51678bf15e97f1891d95ed9dda7e697ef18a593a507171a45e6cc7ffc7e.png)\n\nThis function repeats itself with a period of $2$. To illustrate, first focus on the part of the graph shown in blue below.\n\n![](images/fb/fbba2663aaca4514f0b6132ed36e48d7568637fe0faac327d6935b5f5df65541.png)\n\nIf we shift this part $2$ units to the right, the graph remains unchanged!\n\n![](images/de/deeadebca74eb6e466ebb13240e8f4cf9b0d2c03fedb5f8a94be18f1ddcd44f1.png)\n\nThe same would happen if we shifted the part $2$ units to the left.\n\nBy knowing the period of a periodic function, we can use a value of the function to infer many other values of the function. For example, in the function above, note that\n\n$$\nf(4)=0\n$$\n\nSince the period is $2$, if we add (or subtract) any multiple of $2$ from the input, the output stays the same. We can write this as\n\n$$\nf(4+2n) = 0\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nFor example, setting\n\n$$\nn=1,2,3\\ldots\n$$\n\ngives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn = 1: f(4 + 2(1)) &= f(6) = 0 \\\\\nn = 2: f(4 + 2(2)) &= f(8) = 0 \\\\\nn = 3: f(4 + 2(3)) &= f(10) = 0 \\\\\n&= ⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe same is true for negative values of $n$. Setting\n\n$$\nn=-1,-2,-3\\ldots\n$$\n\ngives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn =-1: f(4 + 2(-1)) &= f(2) = 0 \\\\\nn =-2: f(4 + 2(-2)) &= f(0) = 0 \\\\\nn =-3: f(4 + 2(-3)) &= f(-2) = 0 \\\\\n&= ⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b8020c2e-cfe4-5187-94df-23d7d0fcb605" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1502 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "fa1d756d-c380-5339-a443-11ba085cc909" :step/math-academy-id 3664 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134882}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134882 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133902 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133469 :question/id #uuid "9a8dfc78-929c-525b-9403-7c1d11d8713f" :question/math-academy-id "e-1124"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ebe5738-7682-55ad-a21b-235063f08b88" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049534}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234131}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Period of a Function Given Its Graph"} :step/id #uuid "5c25e487-4990-59fc-bfc4-1a9c9ad30723" :step/math-academy-id 3665 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134883}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134883 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133903 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133470 :question/id #uuid "1529ba15-ecf2-5c3f-871d-ab249ef9af34" :question/math-academy-id "e-1125"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57b95b5b-8834-5d1a-8380-fd8889e05cb4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134490}] :knowledge-point/title "Drawing the Graph of a Periodic Function"} :step/id #uuid "e726ab95-f076-53ce-a8d3-da7938f96dec" :step/math-academy-id 3668 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134884}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134884 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133904 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133471 :question/id #uuid "6f991ce1-e19a-5324-830a-a17f7ac22848" :question/math-academy-id "e-1126"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "282b873c-1ba0-5205-b75f-c58721ffaaff" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134491} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234038}] :knowledge-point/title "Inferring Values of a Periodic Function Given Its Graph"} :step/id #uuid "19fd2e5e-dcb6-5935-bb5a-e6645996a5d3" :step/math-academy-id 3671}] :activity/title "Periodic Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134017}]}
